  "final_answer": "I have found the answer. Let me summarize the key findings:\n\n## Repository Found: **bjornbytes/lovr**\n\n**Verification against criteria:**\n- ✅ **Virtual reality related**: Tagged with `virtual-reality`, `vr`, `openvr`, `openxr`, `oculus`, `oculus-quest`\n- ✅ **Updated in the last 10 days**: Updated \"yesterday\" (latest commit \"Merge branch 'stable' into dev\" 4 days ago)\n- ✅ **At least 200 stars**: 2,601 stars (2.6k)\n\n## Main Objective Summary\n\n**LÖVR** is a simple Lua framework for rapidly building **3D games and experiences in virtual reality**. Its key characteristics include:\n\n- **Purpose**: Enables developers to quickly create VR games and applications using Lua scripting\n- **Cross-Platform**: Runs on Windows, macOS, Linux, and Android\n- **Cross-Device**: Supports any VR headset that works with OpenXR (including Oculus/Meta Quest)\n- **Beginner-friendly**: Simple scenes can be created in just a few lines of code\n- **Fast & Lightweight**: Written in C11 with LuaJIT scripting — the entire framework is just a couple of megabytes\n- **Powerful GPU APIs**: Includes compute shaders, ray tracing, indirect rendering, and other modern graphics features\n- **Asset Support**: Imports glTF models, skeletal animation, and compressed textures\n\nIn essence, LÖVR aims to provide an accessible, lightweight, and powerful Lua-based engine that lowers the barrier to entry for VR game development, similar to how LOVE2D simplified 2D game creation in Lua.",
